  explicit [38;5;153mimport[39m is what makes that reuse legible. The [38;5;153mfile://[39m limitation is the one thing that might rule it out, so
  tell me if opening [38;5;153mindex.html[39m directly matters to your workflow.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · How should the shared identity module be wired into the browser app? → ES modules (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Last question, then the design. This is the YAGNI line for the module's surface.

  You've said other forms will need the ID later. What's unclear is whether they just need to [3mread[0m it, or whether they
  need to [3mreact[0m when it changes.

  - [1mMinimal: set / get / clear[0m — three functions. Login stores it, any form reads it synchronously, logout clears it.
    Covers every consumer that just needs to know who's logged in at the moment it runs. Adding more later is easy and
    breaks nothing.
  - [1mPlus change notification[0m — a subscribe callback so UI can re-render on login/logout. Necessary only if something on
    screen must update without a page load. Real cost: subscription lifecycle and unsubscribe bugs, for a benefit no
    current code needs.
  - [1mPlus request integration[0m — a wrapper that attaches the ID to outbound calls automatically. Tempting, but premature:
    [38;5;153mlogin()[39m is still a stub that never makes a request, so there's nothing to attach to yet, and a client-set ID header
    is not something a server should trust as identity anyway.

  My recommendation is minimal. Everything here is additive later, and the two larger options both want a consumer that
  doesn't exist yet to tell you their shape.
